...,
This message wasnt intended for mass consumption. Anyone who has
seen me post before knows I am not in the business of character
assassination. The comments I made we're over stated and a precursor
to another problem that has bothering me for some time of which this
incident has set off. I sincerely apologize if I have offended anyone in any
way from what I wrote, but I stand by my opinion and here's why.

Despite what some people may think the Alpha/linux community has been
growing,
not at an exponential rate but growing none the less. There are outstanding
issues that continue to affect the Alpha port like X's
performance/compatibility,
linux driver compat, and linux in general. Alot of these bugs have become
accepted as defacto, This should not be the case. Alot of alphalinux users
seem
to wrestle with some of these problems and others that are unknown to the
majority on their own and keep quiet about their mishaps or sometimes their
success.

Ladies and Gentlemen, This list shouldnt be this quiet considering
what is outstanding. Why are not users asking more questions before striking
out on their own? Those of us who have the capacity and the desire to help
cannot be effective if not presented with the problem. Moreover, The lack of
coordination between users/developers and upstream sources is broken.

I propose a general laundry list of what needs to be fixed on linuxalpha.
It doesn't have to be distribution specific since the bugs in all likley
hood would affect all. All interested parties could refer to this list/
database and work on the problem independently or in groups. Reporting
their results back to the 'main' list and fixing the problem in their
domain as well. Whether that would be as a package manager,upstream
developer,
or rogue developer. The protocol will need to be expanded and detailed
but the system can work, I think it can.

I would like to do some work on milo and (as you might as well guessed) X.
Anyone else with a similar interest in these areas please contact me and
lets see what we can collaborate on. As I said before, I have never
been surrounded by a group of individuals whos expertise and wisdom is
as vast as those individuals who make up this 'the' axp-list. I believe
if only few more folks could get together and direct their efforts.
The Linux/Alpha port could be what I believe and I hope others too should
be,
second to none.

Again, I sincerely apologize if I offended anyone in anyway. It was intended
as
a private message but alas wasnt. Moreover the tone I set was 'short' and
inexcusable. I apologize. Good night.

Peter
