Here's my $0.02,

	I'm still new at this alpha thing, but after my trials and
reading Peter's email, I have come up with a few suggestions.

	A working 'laundry list' should have at least the following:

	1) Good GNOME/KDE porting.  I hate having to wait for a new
distribution of linux alpha to be able to use GNOME.  For some strange
reason, people developing this desktop use platform specific code that
either won't compile (after passing the ./config) or continuously
crashes and hangs the system.  To name just one example, I compiled the
latest version of Xchat, and it does something ungodly to the CPU usage
and crashes X.  KDE won't even compile at all, even if all dependencies
are met.

	2) Out of the Box USB installation.  After 3 weeks of fighting
with USB, something in the driver is either conflicting with my other
PCI devices, or, after the USB card is recognized, it can't communicate
with the devices connected to it.  The USB documentation says that I may
need to pass some bizarre parameter to the kernel at boot time, but
everything is quite unhappy.  (And this is using the latest 2.4.17
kernel.)

	3)  A well documented how-to on getting sources to compile with
Compaq's C compiler.  Compaq claims a huge performance gain over
GCC/G++, but so far I have met only limited success at getting things to
compile.  Before anyone tells me to RTFM, I have read the FAQs and
documentation is pretty darn spotty.

	4) DOCUMENTATION, DOCUMENTATION, DOCUMENTATION!  This is quite
possibly the largest problem with alpha linux.  The alphalinux.org site
has a bare minimum of information on dealing with the idiosyncrasies of
alphas.  For me, jumping the the alpha platform from only using x86
hardware was quite a shock.  We need information and lots of it, and not
just from an expert's point of view.  FAQ's should be established
dealing with each different alpha platform, the common issues associated
with it, and the solutions.

Ted Goodridge

	

