> From: "Chuck Taylor" <cdtaylor51@hotmail.com>
> Date: Fri, 1 Mar 2002 22:02:02 -0800
>
> I have installed RedHat 7.1 on my PWS600a/au.  ...
>
> I now have:
>     RedHat Linux 7.1 on one disk
>     Windows NT 4.0 on a second disk
>     Tru64 5.1a on a third disk
>     OpenVMS 7.3 on a fourth disk
>
> I am a pretty happy camper right now.  I am now looking for training on
> Linux.  I have already found some things but I am open to suggestions.

Four operating systems on one machine.  Impressive!

I would guess you are probably already aware of this, but if
you are switching back and forth between SRM and AlphaBIOS,
they reportedly use different real time clock formats, which
can cause the year to be off by a few decades.

One suggestion for training is to consider subscribing to
Linux Journal.  They have a web site, so you could check it
out to see whether the content looks interesting to you.

I have a web browser to http://www.google.com/linux and find
it useful when searching for Linux-related stuff.

One system configuration idea you might consider is installing
multiple repetitions of Linux.  I only have Linux on my box,
but I have three installations on two disk drives.  Each disk
has between 5 and 8 partitions.  The first is /boot.  The
second is a standalone Linux installation.  The third is
swap.  The rest of the partitions on both disks are various
parts of the tree.  I make sure to put /usr/local and /home
on their own partitions.

With different aboot flags, I can boot either of the standalone
installations of the main (partitioned) installation.  That way
I have a backup path in case of irreparable file system
corruption or failure of a physical disk.  Most of the time, I
keep the standalone partitions unmounted when running the main
installation, but when doing nightly backups I mount each and
copy the backup tarballs to them.

When upgrading to a new Linux version, I install on one of the
standalone installations first, so I can fall back to the main
installation if the upgrade goes poorly.  After upgrading, I
can keep the old version on the other standalone installation
for a while in case I need to fall back.  One thing I learned
is to do a straight install on the main, partitioned installation
rather than copy from the standalone to the partitioned one.
Some files are hard links between /bin and /usr/bin, and this
does not work when copying from one partition to several.
Because I keep /home and /usr/local on their own partitions,
I retain them (mostly) unmodified across OS upgrades.

Have fun.  :-)

Robert Riches
