Start with updates to your system.  They can be found on
ftp://updates.redhat.com (overloaded hence slow) and various mirrors.
There is an array of security fixes and you will get also a
substantially better kernel in a bargain.  These are current updates:

    9408837 Feb 27 08:37 kernel-2.4.9-31.alpha.rpm
    5486672 Feb 27 08:37 kernel-BOOT-2.4.9-31.alpha.rpm
    1613451 Feb 27 08:37 kernel-doc-2.4.9-31.alpha.rpm
    1143785 Feb 27 08:37 kernel-headers-2.4.9-31.alpha.rpm
    9691562 Feb 27 08:37 kernel-smp-2.4.9-31.alpha.rpm
   24692830 Feb 27 08:38 kernel-source-2.4.9-31.alpha.rpm


> I even got to
> observe a power-loss crash of my system due to an unexpected  power
> interruption.  Very impressive that the system verifies and fixes any
> corruption that has happened in the file system.

You may be even more impressed when you convert some (all?) your file
systems to ext3 (possible to convert there and back to ext2 without
reformatting your partitions and loosing any data with a suitable
kernel and high enough version of e2fsprogs) and it will just reply
for you journal and recover. :-)

If you converted make sure that a kernel you are booting either has
a support for ext3 builtin or you are loading a needed module in
your initrd or you will have some troubles starting your machine.

> I now have:
>     RedHat Linux 7.1 on one disk
>     Windows NT 4.0 on a second disk
>     Tru64 5.1a on a third disk
>     OpenVMS 7.3 on a fourth disk
> 
> I am a pretty happy camper right now.  I am now looking for training on
> Linux.  I have already found some things but I am open to suggestions.

Believe or not on a general level it is not that much different from
your Tru64.  There is a bunch of details and some tools are available
on one side but not another, or you will find something similar but
under a different name, but these are just details.

There is quite a few books on Linux around and save such topics like
steps in a bootup sequence they are not really processor specific.

  Michal

p.s. To be sure 'ext3', or any other journaling system, totally
eliminates fsck but it just makes it used much less.  It is good
idea to force filesystem checks after a series of unclean shutdowns
or some spectacular one. :-)  fsck will be called automatically in
any case if a file system was not checked for a long time or mounted
more than a given number of times (both tunable with tune2fs).




