> difficulty of installing RedHat 7.1 on Milo only machines. I've
> seen comments like, upgrade to SRM and certainly on systems I
> have that can run SRM, I've moved to but we have to realize that
> there are lots of Milo only machines out there.

Considering that the majority of Alphas running Linux (or not running DUNIX)
out there are pre ev6. Their SRM is no longer supported by Compaq and
upgrading to SRM is more of a downgrade than anything. Linuxalpha
machines should not be held back by the constraints of SRM console.
MILO can be polished to the point where it can be the standard
console not the alternative. The biggest sticking points I see for this
are availability of proper PAL code (SRM w/ timings )and BIOS emulation.
Both of which Compaq could provide in binary and encoded form under EBSDK
license. Once that's done, there's no reason not to use milo. Does
anyone have any other sticking points that would prevent you from
running milo?

> Now I know it's not too difficult for someone who knows what to
> do to build a new kernel for the installation process provided
> you have another working alpha system, but it's a bit chicken and
> egg for someone who has picked up an Alpha for a few dollars to
> play with, only to discover (probably after lots of time and
> effort) that they cannot install on their box.

Exactly.

> I'm more than happy to test and contribute a new kernel and Milo
> setup for an XL366 and a UDB if anyone needs one. Perhaps we
> could setup a repository of suitable boot and root disks? We will
> also need to create suitable documentation to explain how to do
> the install.

Anything you and anyone else could contribute is usefull, the more
bits the better. We'll see what we can do with ALO about a repository,
we don't have FTP currently. Documentation needs alot of work. I am willing
to work on that and milo itself.

