As I recall the official party line was that AlphaBIOS was being scrapped for
SRM
Then again there was wisperings of LinuxBIOS working on alpha.

The main reason that I can see for using AlphaBIOS is to get at the x86
emulation so that you can latch into the onboard BIOS config tools that the
like of SCSI cards tend to have. I doubt LinuxBIOS has that ability though I'm
reasonably sure that some nutter will go out of their way to add it. As for
SRM, I don't know too much about the internals of it or how much BIOS emulation
would cost in terms of size for it to squeeze into onboard prom size.

Another major differance is the default disk partitioning schemes,.. SRM knows
BSD Label and AlphaBIOS knows MSDOS but neither know both. BSDLabel offers up
to 8 partitions whereas AlphaBIOS can be coerced into provideing 12 per disk (
I think thats about as far as I managed to trick it into creating). An
additional advantage of MSDOS partitioning is that you can change the partition
type to one of the supported RAID types (fd) and start playing with software
raid (0,1,5) though you can kinda do that with SRM if you remember to keep just
the BOOT disk in BSDLabel format, theres no reason why all the rest can't be
MSDOS type. (innocent whistle)


