On Mon, Mar 04, 2002 at 03:31:42PM -0500, 'Bryce' wrote:
> 
> Another major differance is the default disk partitioning schemes,.. SRM knows
> BSD Label and AlphaBIOS knows MSDOS but neither know both. BSDLabel offers up
> to 8 partitions whereas AlphaBIOS can be coerced into provideing 12 per disk

This is only a boot device requirement imposed by firmware.  On all
other disks you may have any partitioning you want as long as it is
recognized by Linux.  I have actually a running machine with mixed kinds
of partition tables on different disks. :-)  This means in theory up to
15 partitions on a SCSI disk and up to 63 on an IDE one with the current
major-minor device asignments IF your partitioning scheme can accomodate
that.  As far as partition type numbers in BSD label go if you use some
unknown ones to some user-space tools then will be just that - unknown
which does not mean unusable.  This is purely a user-space issue and a
convention.

Stefan Reinauer (http://www.stefan-reinauer.de/Milo/,
http://www.suse.de/~stepan/) was working quite a bit on milo but
I do not know in what stage this project is right now.

  Michal




