> LinuxBIOS 'does' work but it's little more than aboot with some
> hardcoded values attached as a header to the kernel and then made a rom
> image.
> Flashed in and away it goes. API wrote DBLX http://dblx.sf.net which does

just poked there. It shows up as empty

> what the LinuxBIOS project does, but saner. With a firmware
> that can manage flash and booting Linux from flash. For the
> majority of users I don't see any real benefits gained from
> booting Linux directly out of flash save for those folks who
> have clusters of machines, which is what DBLX was targeted at.
> 
> I have a few points of contention against LinuxBIOS/DBLX like
> you have to actually play with flash to upgrade your kernel,
> That is inherently dangerous. On all Alphas the possibility
> exists where you could blow away the FSB. On systems like
> the UP1000 and CS20 you could destroy the SROM which would
> make the machine a paperweight without expert intervention.

My impression of LinuxBIOS was that it could be used to boot a second
kernel and that this second kernel would be the one that is changed.
Reflashing every time you change kernels would be too silly.  MILO is in
some respects the same thing a linux kernel with some glue around it to
make it flashable. 

I have SRM, MILO and Alphabios running here. I would love dearly to be
able to go to a single boot loader but with various generations of alpha
hardware and some odd peripherals. I am stuck with using what ever works
and suffering the side effects.

> 
