"... been hit
indirectly and directly with a lot of negative commentary lately
from various places,..."

This may seem off-topic, but bear with me.

Over the years I have put together housing programs from thin air that
built at least 200 houses for people who were living in shacks, dugout
holes in the ground, brush shelters, car bodies or who had no homes at
all.  These were all built by scrounging public money for materials and
hiring unemployed skilled (well, some more than others) labor  .. we
built good houses, and we built a lot of them because we weren't paying
the financiers and contractors' rake-off, and we weren't raking anything
off ourselves.  Socialism is GOOD!

You would think that doing something like that would make people happy,
that they would appreciate your efforts ... and mostly it was true.  But
some people got houses and some people did not.  A lot of those who
didn't get houses, even if they had pretty good houses of their own,
complained loudly.

Some of the people who got houses didn't like something about their
houses, and THEY complained.  

The complainers were always a certain percentage of the number of people
we served, so-o-o-o

The more housing we built, the more people we helped, the more
complaints came in, and the louser they were.  Inevitably the
politicians got involved decided to "clean up the mess in housing" ...
and pretty soon it was no longer possible to build houses.  

I think an open source software developer is in a similar position as a
provider of public goods.  The more work you do, the more important it
is and the better you do it ...  the MORE people will complain. The more
complainers there are the more noticable becomes the small percentage
who are truly nasty and vicious.   You almost never hear from the people
who are happy and satisfied.  

Why is it like that?

I don't know.

jn





