Content-Type: text/plain;
	charset="us-ascii"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit

The fail safe switch is a jumper you place.  The exact location should
be in the manual.  When the jumper is set on bootup, it reads a floppy
which you should put the ROM image on.  Beware, however, you may lose
your graphics console.  Be prepared to connect via the serial port if
necessary (such was my experience).
 
Ted Goodridge
 
-----Original Message-----
From: axp-list-admin@redhat.com [mailto:axp-list-admin@redhat.com] On
Behalf Of Ben Ryall
Sent: Thursday, March 07, 2002 11:58 AM
To: axp-list@redhat.com
Subject: AlphaBios and 'Invalid NVRAM data' Error
 
Hi all,

I've got an Alphastation 200 4/166 which I was given and hoping to put
redhat on to. When I power it up it boots into AlphaBios and after all
the checks has a message on the screen saying 'Invalid NVRAM data'

Anyone have any ideas - I read in a similar mail about a 'fail-safe boot
switch' - not having any instruction books does mine have one of these?
 
I've downloaded the latest SRM from the DEC site and made a disk read,
can't get it to work as yet thou.

Many thanks in advance,

Cheers,
Ben
 

