"\"Thursday\" AKA dr. john" wrote:

> This is a follow-up.
>
> I have been running the 3DLabs Oxygen VX1 video card on my PWS500a/u for
> about a month now, under RedHat 7.1, Tru64 5.1, T64 5.1a, Windows NT and
> lately, Windows 2000.
>
> I had a real problem under RH 7.1 with a rythmic, 2-second-repeating
> blackout. Sometimes I got it, sometimes not ... it was driving me nutz.
> Setting the resolution down to 1280x1024 helped a little ..
>
> Here's what I have found.  I noticed that when my machine got the "VX1
> shakes" it seemed also to lose the date in the BIOS clock. The date
> would default to early in the year 2050.
>
> I started doing a "date" at SRM prompt and resetting it if it was
> wrong.  No more shakes.
>
> I experimented: I could get the VX1 shakes any time I wanted to by
> letting the TOY (BIOS) clock slip (My Tru64 always sends the date back
> to the future when it shuts down).  As loong as the date remained at
> 2050, the shakes would occur.  reste the date, the shakes go away.
>
> There has gotta be some kind of Masonic handshake between the OS and the
> TOY clock, "bye-bye, shutdown, it is now the year 2050 (or 2022 from my
> Windows 2k if I set the OS date current).
>
> I had been thinking it was a hardware problem, a wormy chip that would
> randomly lose track of its stuff and reset to some default.  Below I
> have attached an earlier post ...
>
> This whole biz raises more issues than it resolves.  I set the TOY clock
> 20 years ahead when I installed the supposedly time-bombed RC1 version
> of Windows2000 ... it boots and tells me thjat I should set the date
> current, when I do, my box adds 20 years to the date on shutdown.
>
> I didn't check the console date when I installed T64 5.1a.  Maybe it had
> gone to 2050?  I did reset the clock in the OS and it keeps time expcept
> for being 2 weeks behind ...
>
> Below is my last posting on this issue .. I got mail back suggesting it
> was the southbridge.  People at Compaq suggested there was a weak
> chipset (the Pyxis) in the PWS machines.
>
> Maybe it is a combination of hardware, software and operator error?  I'm
> gonna try some selective OS reinstallations and see what happens. I am
> not so far into either W2K or T64 that I can't rip them out and start
> over.
>
> jn
>
> ""Well the last 2 days I had the VX1 shakes again, even at the reduced,
> 1280x1024 resolution.
>
> It happened again today.  So I spent a couple hours goofin around with
> the computer running, then rebooted RedHat, and hey! no problems with
> the video card!
>
> Also today, i noticed the date clock had reset itself to the 2050 year
> default setting ...  and I had some hot-off-the-net firmware on a floppy
> that I thought might help.
>
> This was a FAT-formatted floppy, my plan was to use the Alpha Bios to
> install, mainly because I'd been having zero luck with any other
> floppies, like off my PC Unix box, or downloads from my system at work.
>
> Besides the date being wrong, the Alpha Bios couldn't see/read the FAT
> floppy.  Add it up:
>
> 1)      To install RedHat, I had to run the computer 24 hours straight
> before
> i could boot/install from floppies
>
> 2)      All the problems reading CDs, and having to change CDrom drives
> to
> install RedHat from the CD no. 1
>
> 3)      The date is all goosey.  I put in a new battery, within a few
> days
> the date slips again.
>
> 4)      (Maybe) The video card works sometimes, sometimes it doesn't.
> There
> is no obvious pattern, but if tyhe computer has run a couple hours, it
> seems more likely to hook up the card on boot.
>
> 5)      The Firmware is rock steady through all this, no corrupted
> screens,
> no reverting to previous versions, no hang-ups.
>
> 6)      There are messages all over this list and the Tru64 Unix
> managers
> list about VX1 problems, about problems installing RedHat from CDroms,
> and from people who can't get an installation going from floppies.
>
> So, Watson, what do you deduce?
>
> I ask:  how likely is it that a Cdrom drive, a floppy dirve, and a new
> battery would ALL go south at the same time?  How likely is it that 2
> different OSes would have similar problems with hardware?
>
> My own thought is that there is something rotten at or upstream of the
> controller for the floppy drive, for the IDE drive, and for the clock.
> Further, I would suggest that this is a problem specific to the PWS
> series ...  maybe to a certain run of motherboards or backplanes. The
> question is, how does this affect the connection with the Video Card on
> the PCI bus?  Or is it completely separate?
>
> Elementary, NOT!
>
> jn""
