from my point of view Win2k on, for example, an AS/8400 was far superior to
win2k on any Wimptel server.  Much faster bus and expandability, never mind
throwing oodles of CPU's on it.  granted normal everyday applications might
run a little slower due to the size of the code and might give the false
impression Intel chips are faster then Alphas, not the case.  if you run
optimized code for the class CPU (like EV56 or EV6 instructions only instead
of EV4 base instructions with EV56 additions to make it compatible with all
alphaservers) then things will be somewhat faster, esp. for number crunching
and such.  also, the new EV6 and above CPUs issues 4+ instructions per clock
cycle while the Pentiums are still at 2 (could be mistaken with the P4), not
including the EV7 which is far ahead of anything Intel has out currently.
but then again, the Alpha was never meant to be a desktop CPU besides the
failed attempt with the PCA56/PCA57 CPUs.  then again, Pentiums ARE
Alphas!!!!  Intel stole some stuff from Alphas and Digital sued them.
hmmm...

and i still have a CD of Windows NT 5.0 Beta 1...THAT should have been never
released or called "Build #1 Internal". UGH!

but i agree, why would someone want to waste a good AS8400/GS160 or whatever
on a M$ application that is bloated and buggy anywayz?  UNIX rules!  then
again there is SUN SLOWaris. ;-)

