> On Thu, Mar 07, 2002 at 01:42:53PM -0600, Ted Goodridge, Jr wrote:
> > I want to compile X and my kernel with CCC, how do I go about doing
> > this?  This has been discussed in the list before, and I was hoping
> I
> > might start compiling a FAQ.
> 
> First you are getting an access to CCC sources (good luck with this).
> Next you are implemeting all gcc extensions which are used by kernel
> and
> you also modify a parser, while you are at it, to accept an assembler
> syntax as used by gcc.  After you did all of that, and thorougly
> debugged it and made sure that all silent expectations about
> generated
> code are met, you compile your kernel to find out that results of all
> this exercise are likely slower (there are good reasons for that
> prognosis) although you probably hope that differences are not really
> measurable.  If the later is the case you did _really_ well.
> Congratulations!
> 
> As for X server most likely just change make sure that CC variable in
> all Makefiles is set to 'ccc' instead of 'gcc'.  There is a top
> configuration file which should be enough.  Maybe you need adjust a
> bit
> option strings.  If you will see any overall performance gains from
> that
> then please let us know. I mean for the system and not some well
> chosen
> program or benchmark.
> 
>   Michal
