I, myself would:

1)	Take the src RPM from redhat, install the source, and rpm -bc on the
.spec file in redhat/SPEC
2)	Once you know that you have the X/spec/rpm file working ( i think you
will need some 500meg for it all ), you can edit the spec file. Your
would in essence add in the makefile build flags "CC=ccc CFLAGS=-O2 --
and whatever other ccc flags ) in the build section . Hopefully that
will build all the makefiles ( via ./configure ) with ccc as the c
compiler, and your CFLAGS as your options. 
3)	do again the rpm -bc for the altered spec file.
4)	Somewhere along the way, there is going to be some 'link'ing, which
may not be what ccc needs to get it going. So I suspect its going to
bomb.
5)		repeat steps 3,4,5 as often as necessary to make it go the way you
want. You might also want to look at the src's for a Digital Unix port
-- maybe the flags, and linkages will be of help there.

goo luck, its going to be time consuming, so see ya in april :-}
/gat


