On Sun, Mar 10, 2002 at 04:34:14PM -0500, Peter Petrakis wrote:
>
> Considering the age of the system the SCSI controller probably
> maxes at SCSI-3 40MB/s. I seriously doubt it is LVD capible.
> I have an ISP1040 and it isnt LVD capible so I don't think
> the 1020 would be either.

Correct. Again, consulting the appropriate info at:

	http://www.qlogic.com/products/pci_scsi_products_controllers.asp

we see that not until the 1080/1280 series did they have Ultra2/LVD.

In fact, it appears that QLogic has been, um...well, *logical* when it
comes to the naming of their controllers... ;-}

For example, the 1040/1240 series max out at 40MB/sec, ie Ultra Wide.
The 1080/1280 series, at 80MB/sec, U2W. And 10160/12160 at 160MB/sec,
Ultra3 Wide. Etc, etc. The 1020 doesn't appear in the table, but we
assume it's name is indicative of 20MB/sec max, ie fast and wide only.

(Note: the difference between 1040 and 1240 is, that 1040 is single
channel, while 1240 is dual channel.)

> When the kernel boots it will tell
> you what mode your disks have negotiated at. If you miss it
> (it goes by fast) you can later go through dmesg and see
> the output there. Hope that helps.

Unfortunately, dmesg won't help; I think you've been looking at the
Adaptec and NCR/LSI/Symbios driver's output too much, Pete... ;-}

I don't believe the stock QLogic driver in the Linux kernels has ever
printed a message about the speed(s) it has negotiated. I think we've
always just hoped that the driver would do the best it can, and that
that is at the level of the best that the controller can do.

A better driver, downloadable from www.feral.com (hi, Matt :-),
does print the negotiated speed(s). Very much worth a try if you
have any problems with the stock driver.

Good luck.

--Jay++

