I'm working on getting a 'wiki' setup on linuxalpha.org to organize this and
other things like a nice compiling with ccc faq :-). I don't know if all of
you are familiar with a wiki, It's basicly a FAQ board that is added to and
updated by users.
Here's a good example, http://wiki.fresco.org/index.cgi/FrontPage . My hopes
are that users will populate the boards and we (ALO) can then take that
information and distill it into a static FAQ/HOWTO while still linking to
the wiki.

Onto the other points you raised. I agree with what you said about the FX!32
patent issues. Also from what I've heard there much of a FX!32 team left at
Compaq so even if there where an effort put forth to seperate the code
covered by patent and not, the work would prohibitive to those who are left.
That's my observation, not fact. Someone disagree with me, please :-). Aside
from that IMHO that we no longer need x86 compatibilty. You can still buy
applixware for Alpha as AFAIK and at the rate koffice and openoffice are
comming along a compariable replacement to applix wont be far off. There's
also siag office which I have yet to try. For web browsers, I use mozilla
.98 on my deb box 99% of the time. I don't bother with the mail portion
though the web browser is pretty solid. The 64 bit clean issue may soon be a
thing of the past when the "I wanna be 'lite with my 64 bit clawhammer too'
group discover what doesnt work in userland with 64 bits :-). I think that
covers most of the reasons why someone would want x86 compat.

With regards to firewire I'm interested in that as well. I'm considering the
pro's
and con's of replacing a SCSI subsystem totally with a firewire one.

Peter
