I have had partial success with this in the past but the contortions
I had to go through to get it past the point of where it failed
was down right midevil. basicly there is a single tool that
fails to build before you even get to the actual X sources. I don't remember
how I worked around it but it wasnt easy :-) and I never did achieve a
successfull
clean compile with ccc. I had unresolved symbols that had no business being
unresolved like printf. The errors messages for when that ancient tool
failed to compile where indecipherable to me at the time (6+ months ago). If
anyone wants to take a crack at it simply change the HOSTCC (i think thats
it) option in the host.def file to ccc and let it run. It will fail rather
quickly. Those of you who
are unfamiliar with compiling X from scratch I recommend that you take a
look at the nice spec files that Mike has in his personal RPMS and the DRI
project has a nice little tutorial. ftp://people.redhat.com/mharris/ and
http://dri.sourceforge.net/doc/DRIcompile.html respectivly.

Peter
