> Hello Wes,
> 
> On Alpha systems most of my applications are cluster related and customer
> specific, so I can not really chat about them.  Overall, I do see about a 20%
> integer performance improvement with ccc over gcc 2.96.x.  I believe that gcc
> 3.1.x when released may have improved integer performance on Alpha.
> 
> I usually use:
> 
> ccc -arch ev6 -fast -O4 ONESTEP
> 
> -arch ev6
>         I do not want to use the ev4/5 instruction set nor scheduling.
> 
> -fast
>            Make certain that ccc is using the byte/word instructions.
> 
> 

No surprises.

> As an example, I downloaded nbench 2.1 from
> http://www.tux.org/~mayer/linux/bmark.html and tried it against gcc 2.96 on a
> RH 7.0 system running on a Digital Personal Workstation 500a (EV5/6 500).
> Using the ccc compiler, but adding -fast, and using gcc with -O5 I get:
> 

All I'll say about this benchmark is it's not what I run in production
and is not the most well tuned code around.

Everything I build has been tuned against a minimum of 4 computer architectures.
Ultrasparc/Alpha/POWER3+/P4/Athlon XP are used currently. I do mean heavily checked
and rechecked with a bias toward the machine that currently dominates my production
environment. I'll recode an algorithm if I have to to make it run well.

X is sufficiently mature to have gone through as much tuning as I normally do
on my codes so I expect X's performance characteristics to be similar to my
observations, not nbench's behavior.

What I mean by that is that I've incorporated tricks the compiler would use
into the source so it leaves little left for the compiler to do. The main things
I expect a compiler to do are instruction scheduling and efficient memory access.
A good math lib is nice too.

Things like common sub-expression elimination, loop reordering, constants moved 
out of loops, loop unrolling, etc., are already in the source code.


Wes




