> Until you run a profile against X and Xlibs, it would be hard to say

That is going to be alot work in just getting the backstory on well
those things 'should' work. Unless you're an Xfree developer.

> what's really wrong. Unfortunately, I think the current architecture
> of X makes profiling hard to do because it does dynamic linking of
> modules. Anyone ever try turn on "-pg" and see what's up? Could also

Nope, not yet.

> try attaching gdb to X and starting up the offending program and see
> where X is at by interrupting and looking at what routines it's
> spending time in. Have to have non-stripped binaries though and those
> dynamic links may still be hard to track. Also, you probably want to
> login from another system and do the gdb since the screen will be
> non-responsive when you interrupt X.

I'm familiar with these techniuqes from work with E.

> As to comparing an Alpha to x86, Couple possible reasons. First,
> I'd bet there is some asm floating around for x86. Second, whether you

Yes definiatly and next to none on the Alpha side. Probably not even MVI.
There's no good reason why someone (I guess me since I'm sooooo interested)
couldnt write a set of MVI macros for pre EV6 machines. They're matrix
operations for the most part and as long as the macros are schedualed for
their respective processors there will be a performance gain. Actually,
one could implement that in the kernel like the EV5* compat patch
(to run Civ CTP :-)) that implemented MVI for all Alphas.

> like it or not, Mhz do matter. Have you compared the performance of a
> PIII 600Mhz to your 600Mhz Alpha and it still loses badly? I would expect

I'm losing against my P "II" 400 (no katmai) both with identical video
cards,
voodoo3s. The 2D performance is night and day. Atleast I notice it.

> a P4 or Athlon with clock rates in excess of a Ghz to have no problem
> beating your Alpha or my XP1000's for that matter.

Agreed though I think a properly profiled and optimized X on EV6
that is leveraging MVI will eat thier lunch and then some.

