> 
> Hello Wes,
> 
> I was not attempting to benchmark Alpha(s).   I ran Nbench on an Alpha system
> that was built in 1997.  At that time you where asking about compiler
> performance.  (ccc vs. gcc)
> 
> 1) There are much better benchmarks for system performance.  Please lookup
> the SPEC2000 results.
> 

See other post. The point is to be careful with benchmarks, even on the same
machine with different compilers. It may not represent anything useful for
the true application you're interested in, X in this case. If you do a
similar test with X code, that will be enlightening. You may be correct
that ccc helps 20% as you've seen in your real codes. Even this benchmark
doesn't track your own described experience from what you mentioned previously.

> 2) You are comparing a NEW Athlon 1.4 Ghz against an OLD  Alpha
> system.  For a real comparison, you should use a NEW Alpha system.
> 

Not interested in spending that kind of $$$$$. P4's give too much 
bang for the buck at the moment for what I do.

As to the progress building X with ccc. Consider building it with gcc
first, including "-pg" and see if you can pinpoint where the resource
problems are. Then go back and build those routines with ccc. It can
easily turn out that only a few routines are a real problem.


Wes

