On Fri, Mar 15, 2002 at 10:14:50PM -0700, Rajiv Prasad wrote:
> 
> I installed RH6.1

An error!  If you want to keep within 6.x installations you should
use RH 6.2 plus updates.  There is a qualitative difference.

> on my SX164 because 7.x would not install

Eh?  AFAIK RH 7.0 should install without any problems and RH 7.1
should install as well provided you will use boot/ramdisk pair
of floppies which come with 7.0 and you will rebuild yourself a kernel
configured for MILO afterwards.  You need
CONFIG_ALPHA_LEGACY_START_ADDRESS=y
in a configuration of a kernel meant to boot from milo.

You also should not try milo images on that CD (most likely they are
broken) but use that version about which you know that it really works
on your hardware.

Or there is something special about SX164 which I do not know.

> (MILO problems, and *have* to keep NT4.0 for now).

Agreed about problems but a wrong way of solving these.

> Now I'm trying to upgrade
> the installation as much as possible.

All of this is possible but a fair amount of work.  Kernel sources
come with a file Documentation/Changes which list _minimum_ requirements
on a user space for a given kernel (it happens from time to time
that somebody forgot to update that and these minima are a bit on
low side but usually not a problem; sometimes they are too high).

> First off, downloaded and compiled binutils 2.12.90.0.1.  It built
> without any problems, and I installed it in /usr/local.

If you do that in /usr/local/ (why?) then you have to rebuild your
compiler front end, and possibly modify 'specs' file, so _these_ will
be used internally and not your old versions.  You will need a new
compiler anyway as current kernel code may miss various workaround
for old bugs in egcs.  You should really build custom rpms with a new
stuff if you want to keep an integrity of your installation and
updating all of this will be, in the future, your headache.

> 
> Now, the question is how do I go about installing the new kernel and the
> new modutils? 

As usual.  These will not break your old stuff.  But this is far
too little.

> The README says by default it is installed
> in / -- that would blow away my old modutils, right?

It should or you will run into trouble.

> Is it safe to do,  or should I reconfigure to install modutils 2.4.14
> in /usr/local?

The whole project is of that sort that if you have to ask such questions
you should attempt it first only on a "scratch" installation you can
afford to mess up and redo again if something went wrong.  It could be
educational but I would rather resolve minor hiccups in putting RH 7.1
on your hardware.  There are always ways to do that.  If anything else
failed (this is _really_ the last ditch effort) you can mount your disk
on another machine, put some initial installation on it,  move a disk
back and continue from there.  This other machine does not have to be
Alpha, or even have Linux on it, but if it can run rpm that would be of a
great help (yes, rpm is used not only on Linux).  An ability do deal
with an ext2 file system will be necessary so Linux is the most likely
candidate. :-)

  Michal




