On Sun, Mar 17, 2002 at 10:23:10AM -0700, Rajiv Prasad wrote:
> 
> Update on the 7.1 install, though.  Re-reading Michal's reply to my
> earlier post, he said use 7.0 files to build the boot and ramdisks.

Ah, better.  You start reading what others are writing.  This is a good
first step. :-)  Once again - use a milo which you _know_ for sure
that it works on your hardware.  It only has to read and write kernel.

Oh, make sure that later your kernels to load by milo will reside
on a partition which does NOT have a "sparse" feature as older kernels,
hence milos, will not know how to read such file system.  Keeping a boot
partition "small" will ensure that automatically (or you may specify
that with options to mke2fs).  Another possibility is to keep boot
images on a FAT partition, which definitely will be not "sparse", :-)
but this limits your file names and there is no links.  Not a killer
but sometimes PITA.

> Using 7.0
> boot and ramdisks, and using my old 2.0.35 MILO, I can get the installer
> to start (whoo-hoo!).  Not so fast, though: the 7.0 installer would not
> recognize 7.1 CDs in my CD-ROM drives (neither the Toshiba CD-ROM, nor
> the CD-RW).

This  I do not know.  I _heard_ that there are some boards/firmware
combinations which are unhappy with CDs for some reasons.  If your
CDs are burned from iso images then could be also a question of media.
I have seen on some occassions that _some_ CD readers refuse to read
_some_ CD writeable media.  This is easier to encounter with older
CD readears and rewritable disks but it happens on other occasions.

> So I tried using an ftp install, specifying ftp.redhat.com
> as source, and that seems to work.  I aborted the installation at this
> point.

It is easier and more reliable if you can create a server with all this
stuff on your local network.  You just need a disk space for 2 CD worth
of stuff (or maybe one and a half), possibly temporarily, a network
connection and enough priviledges, or help, to serve that over
anonymous ftp or export via NFS.

> 2. where do I go from here?  Is the ftp install the only answer??

If you can do that on a local network then ftp or NFS install is
actually quite fast and easy option.  Also Guus Schenkelaars had some
suggestions.  There are also "screwdriver" solutions.

Whatever you will do you will need a custom kernel to boot that from
milo.

  Michal




