I've got an compaq ide 48x cdrom working in my alpha pws433/600's (miata)
with a generic ide 12x10x32 CD-RW with burn-proof working just fine.

just add   hdX=ide-scsi  to the end of your entries in /boot/etc/aboot.conf
file, where X is your ide drive letter.  I have a Qlogic scsi card and it
causes no problems.  I currently have no ide hard drive, but I believe I
have used it in the past with no problems.  You're basically telling the
kernel to use the ide-scsi driver just for a particular ide drive, so all
the other ide drives should not be affected.

The only problem I have is with kscd crashing when it tries to access
either /dev/cdrom, /dev/hcX, or /dev/scdX.  But I just use grip or xmms to
play cd's, so it doesn't matter much.  Xcdroast works GREAT.

below you can see my aboot.conf file:
0:1/vmlinuz-2.4.18 root=/dev/sda3 hda=ide-scsi
1:1/vmlinuz-2.4.17 root=/dev/sda3 hda=ide-scsi
2:1/vmlinuz-2.4.9-21 root=/dev/sda3 hda=ide-scsi hdc=ide-scsi
3:1/vmlinuz-2.4.3-12 root=/dev/sda3 hda=ide-scsi hdc=ide-scsi
--
