On Thu, 21 Mar 2002, Terry A. Bowling wrote:

>I'm trying to install the xfree4.2 rpms from rawhide.  There was some
>talk recently about reading the "instructions" before installing 4.1
>because there are some special instructions.
>
>Where are those?  I did an rpm -pqil on the rpm and I check everything
>in /usr/X11R6/lib/X11/doc, but nothing there seems to help me.
>
>Just doing an rpm -Uvh says I need the following:
>xinitrc >=3.13
>Glide3 (do I really need this if I don't have a 3dfx card?)

Technically no.  rpmwise, yes.  Long story, not worth getting 
into however, just install Glide3 to meet the dep and forget 
about it is the easy quick solution.

>kernel-drm

Supplied by the kernel package.  You must have a rawhide kernel 
to meet this requirement.

>mkfontdir

Needed for installation of font packages.


>I'm running the kernel-2.4.9-21 that I got from rawhide.  I notice there
>is a kernel-2.4.9.-31HACK on rawhide.

Both kernels are ancient, and neither provide the proper 
kernel-drm version.  DRI will not function properly.

>Does this contain the kernel-drm?  Do I need to compile my own
>kernel?

You need kernel 2.4.18 from rawhide.

>Isn't mkfontdir supposed to come with xfree-4.2-xfs?

Not anymore, it is in XFree86-font-utils now.

>I installed it with --nodeps, but it still says I don't have
>mkfontdir.

Dont use --nodeps, it is very bad for your health.  You're 
basically telling it "I dont care if this fails due to missing 
requirements".  After such installation things fail because now 
the requirements the package explicitly listed that it needed in 
order to function properly - are not there, so it does not 
function properly.


>Please help.  I stupidly installed a couple of the 4.2 packages with
>--nodeps, so now I have no X until I get this fixed.  When will I learn
>:P

After installing the new kernel and its own dependancies, put all
of the XFree86, glut, freetype, xinitrc, Xconfigurator, hwdata 
packages in a single directory and do:

rpm -Uvh *.rpm

Tip of the day: Never ever use --nodeps or --force when 
installing or upgrading rpm packages.  There is always a proper 
way to do it even if it may not be obvious.  If in doubt, ask on 
a mailinglist first to save yourself from frustration.  ;o)

Hope this helps.


