> There are a variety of version numbers that I see from the various
> "show" commands:
>
>     SRM Version V5.5-1 (July 14 1999)
>     Tru64 PALCode version v1.23-8
>     SROM Revision (?): 2.2
>
> So there it sits.

Unfortunetly it isnt 'that' easy to see which bios emulator you're running.
At power on, the video bios will be initialized. Usually it dumps what kind of
card it is etc etc. Immediatly after that text is displayed the version info the
the bios emulator will display itself. Looks something like this on my LX.

BIOS emulation x2.01
Copyright (c) 1993-1998 Digital Equipment Corporation
All rights reserved
Patent pending

and then SRM loads.

> So what of the possibility of putting my ATI card in as a secondary
> video?

Good.

> I noticed when starting the X Server that the screen looks like
> it is running the BIOS, before the server finally takes over. Does XFree86
> really include its own emulator, and can I use that to make it work?

Yes, and yes. You can specify which card for X to use by specifing the PCI
location of the card in the XF86Config, It's documented.

You now have to deal with the logistics problem of not having a console until X
starts. That is you have only one video output to your monitor and it's hooked
up to the second video card. Otherwise you'd be switching connections on your
vid card every time you booted, Or you invest in a small KVM switch :-).

Peter
