After my recent calamity with a power failure, I have suspicions that
part of the reason I lost the /usr partition was the 2.4.18 kernel I
was running. In particular, I found that fsck under 2.4.18 seems to
manufacture more errors then really existed. It wound up trashing the
/usr partition, which was relatively large.

I come to this conclusion from some trial and error. I was able to
patch the remains of /usr back together with fsck under the RedHat
2.4.3-12 kernel, even when fsck under 2.4.18 was giving unreasonable
errors. (I killed the fsck when it started looking really bad.)

One significant difference is that the 2.4.18 kernel has a new driver
for the Symbios SCSI controller (I have an Intraserver card, which is
based on the chipset) but since it ran for a reasonably long time before
the crash without troubles, I doubt that. (The crash was caused by a
power failure.)

So I'm wondering if there is something specific to fsck's requirements
that is causing the stock 2.4.18 kernel to exhibit a bug. It's all
pretty subtle, I'm not even certain its real, but I haven't seen any
error accessing the disk, just broken contents, especially after an
fsck gone wrong.

So I guess I'm wondering if there are any known problems with the
2.4.18 kernel on alpha and the symbios driver therein.

