On Sat, Mar 23, 2002 at 09:46:57AM -0800, Rajiv Prasad wrote:
> 
> Hi folks:
> 
> I decided to take Michal's advice -- again! -- :-) -- and stay away from "rpm 
> --force" -- I'll just be selective upgrading (-Uvh) rpms that I really need 
> to.

Well, no.  In general, as exceptions happen, you _should_ do in a
situation like yours a wholesale upgrading but with '-Fvh', and not with
'--install' or simple '--upgrade', after you resolved these few
dependencies that may give you trouble.  This may require few tries but
it is not that complicated after all.

> 
> Here's another question: my "make modules_install" ends with:
> 
> cd /lib/modules/2.4.19-pre3; \
> mkdir -p pcmcia; \
> find kernel -path '*/pcmcia/*' -name '*.o' | xargs -i -r ln -sf ../{} pcmcia
> if [ -r System.map ]; then /sbin/depmod -ae -F System.map  2.4.19-pre3; fi
> depmod: *** Unresolved symbols in 
> /lib/modules/2.4.19-pre3/kernel/drivers/ide/ide-probe-mod.o
> depmod:         ide_xlate_1024_hook

This means that after some IDE edits somebody fort to export a symbol
in kernel sources.  You have to edit either your kernel/ksyms.c
or arch/alpha/kernel/alpha_ksyms.c.  To find out which you have to
check what really wants this missing symbol and why.  In this case
probably edits will go to the former.

Reports of this sort, preferably with a patch if possible, should be
posted on linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org mailing list as this is exactly
what it is for.  If you will skip that then do not be surprised if your
problem will never be fixed.  You do not have to subscribe to post there
but if you are playing with "pre" kernels then most likely you should.
See also REPORTING-BUGS file in your kernel sources.

   Michal




