On Sat, Mar 23, 2002 at 10:17:26PM -0800, Stephen Williams wrote:
> 
> I come to this conclusion from some trial and error. I was able to
> patch the remains of /usr back together with fsck under the RedHat
> 2.4.3-12 kernel,

To be sure fsck is not a part a kernel.

> One significant difference is that the 2.4.18 kernel has a new driver
> for the Symbios SCSI controller

It is not likely that your problems lie there.  I was testing this
driver (sym2) for quite a while both on Alpha and Intel and neither
I have any complaints nor I recall any other reports of failure.  You
have a Google provided interface to linux-kernel archives and you can
quickly check that yourself.

IDE code could be a more likely candidate here but you are not using
that.

> So I'm wondering if there is something specific to fsck's requirements
> that is causing the stock 2.4.18 kernel to exhibit a bug.

There are "Current Minimal Requirements" on some elements of a user
space listed in Documenation/Changes of your kernel sources.  I assume
that all these are satisfied in your case?  They differ with kernel
versions.

> It's all
> pretty subtle, I'm not even certain its real, but I haven't seen any
> error accessing the disk, just broken contents, especially after an
> fsck gone wrong.

Although ext2 is pretty robust there are no guarantees that a file
system will survive a power failure or other similar violent incidents.
Incidents like that do happen.  Even journaling does not gives you
absolute certainity (and may have other "interesting" failure modes on
its own :-).  Yes, really messed up file system is not a pretty sight.

  Michal




