On Sun, Mar 24, 2002 at 04:36:36PM -0500, Peter Petrakis wrote:
> 
> > Of the above, I'm not sure if the Savage4 will work under XFree86 on
> > a SPARSE box like PC164; the others, I'm pretty sure WILL...
> 
> What is it about SPARSE mapping that prohibits such devices from working?
> Is it possible to work around it? 

Well, the need for SPARSE on some platforms is because the core logic
chipset (not the CPU, but the circuitry that allows access to the PCI
bus) does not support 8 or 16 bit operations, and some device(s) on the
bus require 8 or 16 bit access to its registers/memory.

Just because a platform may require SPARSE accessing doesn't mean that
certain devices are prohibited from working... ;-}

There are primitives available to XFree86 driver writers which will
perform, *tranaparently*, SPARSE access when needed. Only the driver
writer has to *use* them, and they are not normally required on the
platforms upon which most of the drivers are written (ie x86).

So, yes, there *is* a workaround, it's called "code for portability".

:-) :-)

--Jay++

