To the list:

I have a PWS500au set up to triple-boot RedHat 7.1, Tru64 5.1a and NT. 
The *nix OSes boot via SRM, while the NT boots with AlphaBios.

Alpha BIOS (ARC) and SRM are "exactly" 20 years out of phase with each
other.  Resetting the clock in ARC to get the right date causes the SRM
date to go off by 20 years.  Obviously they are talking to each other.  

If the ARC date is different fromt eh NT date loaded into software i get
an error msg.  Correcting the date in ARC gets rid of the error msg, but
of course messes up the date in SRM.

Long as I stick with RedHat and SRM booting things are OK.  

If i boot into Tru64 i get a msg 

"preposterous date in TOY clock, resetting system clock .. "

then when i shutdown Tru64 the SRM clock has been reset to the year
2050. 

I have tried removing and reinstalliung TRU64, making cert the SRM date
is set correctly .. same thing happens.

I have reinstalled "all" of the firmware.  Same old stuff.

I have formatted the NT system drive, then taken out the MoBo and
battery. I put it back in, the system boots to ARC and tells me it has
reset systme clock to Jan 1 1995 (default).  I set the bios to boot into
SRM, the SRM clock is Jan 1 2015, "the same 20 years int the future as
before"

It wouldn't bother me so much, except when the system clock has been
reset to 2050 by TRU64, my Oxygen VX1 video card gets parkinson's
Disease, a rythmic blackout every 2 seconds or so .. and resetting the
clock is a pain.

I think I've done everyhing short of unslodering and replacing MoBo
chips.

Anyone out there on the list got any ideas what this is all about?


jon norstog




