	Hi;

	Hope this isn't too offtopic, but I know there is a ton of
Alpha mobo/system savvy here.

	I just inherited a PC164 motherboard from a company I did 
some pro-bono work for. The system had been sitting in a closet for 
about the past year. I think it might have originally come from Harddata
in Edmonton (I'm in Calgary).

	When I first plugged in and turned it on, I got sequences of
six beeps eminating from the speaker. After much reading of PC164
motherboard and user manual pdf's, I figured the problem must be
bad ram. 

	I scrounged 4 16mb parity FPM simms that I *knew* were working
and inserted them in the board (sim0-sim3/j5-j8) and made sure that
jumper J1 was 'on'. Turned the system on and, damn, 6 beeps.

	I then hooked up a serial console to the SROM debug port (j31), 
and powered on. Here's what I got;

21164A.01.02.03.04.05.06.07.08.09.0b.0c.0f.13.14.15.3f.06.3f.06.3f.06.3f
.06.3f.06.3f.06.3f.06.3f.06.3f.06.3f.06.3f.06

	So it looks like the system is dying at/after post code 15. I found
a great PDF that documents the post codes (wntig.pdf);

00 Firmware initialization is complete
01 CPU speed detected
02 CPU speed converted
03 Configuration jumpers read
04 Bcache configuration value computed
05 Bcache control value computed
06 Bcache turned off
07 Memory timing registers written
08 Memory control register written
09 Memory bank 0 register written
0B DRAMs awakened
0C Memory sized and memory bank 0 written
0F Bcache turned on
13 All of memory rewritten (good data parity written)
14 Memory errors cleared; start reading system ROM
15 Loading ROM without SROM decompression
16 Loading ROM using SROM decompression
17 System ROM loaded to memory
18 Icache flush code written to memory
19 CPU errors cleared; jump to system code
20 ISA bus reset

	I have a funny feeling that my system rom has gotten corrupted. Can
anyone confirm this feeling, and if I am right, does anyone have any
information
on howto install a new system rom in my PC164 mobo?

	Sorry about the length, but its quite a detailed problem.

	Cheers and thnx.

	Chris




