OK, Bugzilla is ignoring me so I'll post a summary of the glitches
with 7.1 here, in hopes that they find their way to a bug handler
interested in alphas.

Much of this may be PC164 specific. Once I got the low-level stuff
taken care of, it all is working just fine.

Install:

0) Forget ARC and MILO. Hopeless. The kernels on the cdrom are not
   compatible with milo. This is well known, and not a complaint. ARC
   is dead, and I don't miss the Microsoft copyright notice:-P

1) If the disk you are installing on previously had DOS style
   partitions and not BSD partitions, then fdisk will *not* automatically
   go to BSD mode. You have to do this yourself. This is an error in
   the install documentation.

2) Even if you make a blank partition table and let disk druid do
   partitioning, it will not properly account for aboot or srm
   requirements, in spite of the claims in the documentation. It made
   partitions without leaving room for boot space, and subsequently made
   a mess. Forget disk druid, do it yourself with fdisk.

Once partitioning headaches are behind you, the 7.1 install works
perfectly fine.

After Installation:

1) kernel-2.4.3-12 is unstable. It locks up the machine when there is
   lots of disk activity. These are SCSI disks connected to an Intraserver
   ITI3140UW.

2) kernel-2.4.9-anything will not even boot.

   A 2.4.18 compiled from pristine sources works fine. I haven't tried
   the kernels in the rawhide area.


Other:

I have doubts about the e2fsprogs included with 7.1. This error:

> --
> mke2fs: Attempt to write block from filesystem resulted in short write zeroing
> block xxxxxx at end of filesystem
> ----

has been reported. It doesn't occur on the 2.4.3-12 kernel included
with the disks, but it and other errors do occur when running later
kernels. Problems with fsck on 2.4.18 may have been what caused my
initial calamity (destroyed the /usr partition) in the first place.

Conclusion:

SRM video card limitations are a hassle. This is not RedHat's fault.
Us PC164 users should storm Compaq and demand an updated SRM firmware
that supports all the stuff the later machines get to use:-)

