On Tue, Mar 26, 2002 at 09:55:43AM -0800, Stephen Williams wrote:

Just a few comments..

> 
> 1) If the disk you are installing on previously had DOS style
>    partitions and not BSD partitions, then fdisk will *not* automatically
>    go to BSD mode. You have to do this yourself. This is an error in
>    the install documentation.
> 
> 2) Even if you make a blank partition table and let disk druid do
>    partitioning, it will not properly account for aboot or srm
>    requirements, in spite of the claims in the documentation. It made
>    partitions without leaving room for boot space, and subsequently made
>    a mess. Forget disk druid, do it yourself with fdisk.
> 
> Once partitioning headaches are behind you, the 7.1 install works
> perfectly fine.

Much of this can be worked around by using Compaq's Linux JumpStart
for Alpha. Check here:

http://sourceforge.net/projects/jumpstart

Unfortunately, development seems to have been abandoned on it, but
it's really a life saver sometimes, and makes installing a hard-disk
bootable system on an Alpha pretty easy. Anyone know if Compaq plans
on doing anything more with this?

>    A 2.4.18 compiled from pristine sources works fine. I haven't tried
>    the kernels in the rawhide area.

You probably want to patch up to at least 2.4.19pre1 since due to a
development snafu, a couple patches were left out of 2.4.18. I haven't
updated past 2.4.17, myself, so.. I don't actually know if 2.4.19pre1
compiles on Alpha, but as I recall the left out stuff pertained to
non-Intel architectures, so it might be worth looking into.

--
Ryan Schaefer
rysch@chaank.com




