Stephen P. Brown wrote:

> I few years ago I purchased an Alpha machine that I never had the time 
> to really work (play!) with.  I sold it.  A few days ago I decided, due 
> to my new status of having a good deal of free time (unemployment), to 
> get another DEC Alpha, this time a 433au.  I intend to install Linux of 
> some flavor on it, so, a few questions:

Stephen,

I've got a PWS433a and love it. I used it for years as a Linux 
workstation, running Red Hat and SuSE on it. Now, it's a FreeBSD server.

SRM is definitely the way to go, I believe. My machine was a 433a, not a 
433au, so it had a Adaptec SCSI card that was not recognized by SRM (no 
booting from the SCSI drive, but fine after booting). If you've got a 
433au, it should already have a SRM-recognizable SCSI card (I eventaully 
swapped my Adaptec with a Tekram, after booting from a floppy for a while).

I've not had any problems with either Linux or FreeBSD that weren't 
solved by careful reading of the documentation that came with the 
distros. SuSE comes with a great AXP manual that walks you through 
creating BSD disklabels, etc... I got my Red Hat CD from cheapbytes.

Many people have trouble with the fancy-but-not-well-supported video 
cards that come in the PWSs (the ###au machines might have 
better-supported cards). Mine had a monster vidcard that took up 2 64bit 
PCI slots. It was great for the console, but that was it. I put a 
generic Matrox Millenium II in mine and I've had no trouble running X. 
Getting X going was just like an Intel machine. Check the mailing list 
archives, there have been several dicussions in the past year about 
decent video cards supported on Alpha.

So, I had to swap out both the SCSI card and video card. You might be 
better off on both counts! Take your time, read all the README files, 
ask questions of this great list and be patient -- you'll be up and 
running in no time.


-- 
Michael A. Smith <msmith@code-fu.com>
